you've probably had days where you felt  inspired when ideas just seemed to come  freely and willingly and you've probably  had days where you weren't inspired and  it can feel like a light is turned off  even ideas that were exciting the day  before can seem flat and gray and this  is perhaps one of the most difficult  problems with a creative life what to do  when you've lost that spark one option  is to just wait to wait for that spark  to return but that can feel passive  almost like you're resigning a lot of  people who've based their lives around  creative output have turned to something  fairly simple in order to tackle this  dilemma namely at it it sounds easy but  for those of us that have started and  abandoned so many things we know how  hard it can be you might have to start  just by carving out the time the writer  and Lamont for example advises her  students to pick a regular time each day  to sit down and work even if in the  beginning it just means staring at a  blank page the author Balzac would wake  up at 1:00 in the morning to write for  the next 7 hours straight  that's a little hardcore maybe for you  it's just 20 minutes but the trick is to  do it every day but these habits and  routines don't always have to be  directly related to the work Twyla Tharp  the choreographer and dancer once wrote  routine is as much part of the creative  process as the lightning bolt of  inspiration and maybe more she considers  something as simple as getting into a  cab to the gym each morning is a crucial  part of her creative life creative  people throughout history have found  routines that work for them some of them  pretty straightforward like Stravinsky  who would play about fugue every time he  sat down to work some of them get a bit  odd like counting out exactly 60 coffee  beans for the perfect morning coffee as  Beethoven allegedly did or Ben  Franklin's rigorous daily schedule that  included air bathing where he would sit  nude in the cold morning air and for  some the routines become a bit extreme  the artist Francis Bacon reportedly had  a habit of working  hungover something I don't personally  recommend inventors like Thomas Edison  Tesla and da Vinci hardly slept at night  but made a habit of napping with  regularity and then there's dr. Yeoh  Shira Nakamatsu who patented over 3300  inventions including the floppy disk his  creative process includes listening to  music and then diving underwater and  depriving himself of oxygen but your  routine doesn't have to be that extreme  they can take on many forms maybe a meal  or a milkshake or exercise or even an  object like Charles Darwin's notebook in  which he wrote down everything that he  observed which is impressive for a man  who observed so much from the breeding  patterns of field animals to his  thoughts on the viability of marriage  these routines even if they're simple  can help anchor us in something outside  of ourselves something we can reliably  do even when we're not inspired  something to keep us moving it can help  remind us that the creative life is  sometimes lived through small  repetitions the author Yale dr. o once  wrote writing a novel is like driving a  car at night you can only see as far as  your headlights but you can make the  whole trip that way  so what routines do you have and what do  you use to keep yourself going
 

